{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NuBj8rrqoa2ikf4MvN4GkW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public ABI (matches `nm -D` of the C shared library exactly):\n4\t//!   * `merge_sort`\n5\t//!\n6\t//! The C source declares its helpers `static`, so they are not exported; they\n7\t//! are translated here as private Rust functions with identical semantics.\n8\t//!\n9\t//! Behavioural quirks of the original C are reproduced verbatim (they are not\n10\t//! \"fixed\"), most notably `spritebatch_internal_sprite_less_than_or_equal`,\n11\t//! whose second `if` is unreachable because the first `if` already covers the\n12\t//! `a->sort_bits == b->sort_bits` case.\n13\t\n14\tuse core::ffi::c_int;\n15\t\n16\t/// `typedef struct spritebatch_sprite_t { unsigned long long texture_id; int sort_bits; }`\n17\t///\n18\t/// `#[repr(C)]` gives the same layout as the C struct: size 16, align 8 with\n19\t/// 4 bytes of tail padding on the usual LP64 targets.\n20\t#[repr(C)]\n21\t#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n22\tpub struct spritebatch_sprite_t {\n23\t    pub texture_id: u64,\n24\t    pub sort_bits: c_int,\n25\t}\n26\t\n27\t/// ```c\n28\t/// static int spritebatch_internal_sprite_less_than_or_equal(\n29\t///     spritebatch_sprite_t *a, spritebatch_sprite_t *b) {\n30\t///     if (a->sort_bits <= b->sort_bits) return 1;\n31\t///     if (a->sort_bits == b->sort_bits && a->texture_id <= b->texture_id) return 1;\n32\t///     return 0;\n33\t/// }\n34\t/// ```\n35\t///\n36\t/// Note: the second comparison is dead code in the original C (an equal\n37\t/// `sort_bits` already returns 1 from the first check). It is preserved as-is so\n38\t/// the observable ordering is bit-for-bit identical to the C implementation.\n39\t#[inline]\n40\tfn spritebatch_internal_sprite_less_than_or_equal(\n41\t    a: &spritebatch_sprite_t,\n42\t    b: &spritebatch_sprite_t,\n43\t) -> c_int {\n44\t    if a.sort_bits <= b